<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nottingham</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nottingham is a city in the East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midlands">Midlands</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-trent">River Trent</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-heathen-army">Great Heathen Army</a> wintered there in 867–868, and it became one of the Five Boroughs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danelaw">Danelaw</a> until it was taken by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-elder">Edward the Elder</a> in 918. Charles I raised his standard at Nottingham in 1642 to begin the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-civil-war">English Civil War</a>. It later grew as a centre of lace-making and industry and is linked with the legend of Robin Hood.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nottingham.jpg" alt="Nottingham" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Sutton · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nottingham_Council_House_on_Coronation_Day_-_geograph.org.uk_-_7477109.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Nottingham stands on an area of low hills along the lower valley of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-trent">River Trent</a>, 42 miles south-east of Sheffield and 54 miles north-east of Birmingham. It is surrounded by Sherwood Forest in the north, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nottinghamshire">Nottinghamshire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derbyshire">Derbyshire</a> and Yorkshire Coalfield in the west, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trento">Trent</a> and Belvoir Vales in the east and south. The name comes from the Old English Snotingaham, meaning the homestead of the Snotingas, the family or followers of a man called Snot.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The written history of the settlement begins with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-elder">Edward the Elder</a>, who captured it and built a fortress on the south bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trento">Trent</a>. After the Norman Conquest William the Conqueror built a castle there and entrusted it to William Peverel. From around 1100 it was part of the Diocese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lichfield">Lichfield</a>, and from 1884 of the Diocese of Southwell. Nottingham is historically associated with the lace-making, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bicycle">bicycle</a> and tobacco industries, and the Lace Market keeps streets of red brick warehouses. It was designated a city only in 1897, in celebration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a>'s Diamond Jubilee, although it has no Anglican cathedral.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Nottingham is a city and unitary authority area and the county town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nottinghamshire">Nottinghamshire</a>, with a population of 323,632 at the 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. Its centre is the Old Market Square, dominated by the Council House built in the 1920s. Ye Olde Trip to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, partly built into the cave system beneath Nottingham Castle, is a contender for the title of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>'s oldest pub, supposedly established in 1189. Notts County, formed in 1862, was a founder member of the Football League in 1888. In 2015 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> named Nottingham a City of Literature, citing links with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-byron">Lord Byron</a>, D. H. Lawrence and Alan Sillitoe.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nottingham” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Viking Great Heathen Army wintered at <b>Nottingham</b> in 867–868 and at Repton in 873–874, forcing Burgred to flee to Rome.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mercia">Kingdom of Mercia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Jeremiah Brandreth, who led the Pentrich Rising of 1817, was an unemployed <b>Nottingham</b> stockinger and probably a former Luddite.</blockquote><a href="/movement/luddites">Luddites · People</a></li><li><blockquote>John Gay used it in The Beggar's Opera in 1728; Laurence Sterne printed its score in Tristram Shandy, where Uncle Toby whistles it; it carried the English drinking song &quot;<b>Nottingham</b> Ale&quot;, sung at the launch of the East Indiaman Nottingham in 1787, and a nursery rhyme.</blockquote><a href="/songs/lillibullero">Lillibullero</a></li><li><blockquote>After negotiations proved futile, King Charles I raised the royal standard in <b>Nottingham</b>, beginning the First English Civil War.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-22">22 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After a protest in <b>Nottingham</b> was dispersed, framework knitters broke dozens of stocking frames at Arnold.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-11">11 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Aethelflaed of Mercia and Edward the Elder took Derby, Leicester, <b>Nottingham</b> and Stamford in 917 and 918.</blockquote><a href="/state/five-boroughs-of-the-danelaw">Five Boroughs of the Danelaw</a></li></ul></section>
